WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.627 --> 00:00:05.710
GRACE MOLLISON: I'm Grace.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.990
I'm a Solutions Architect
with the Cloud Platform team.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:12.820
And I'm here to talk
to you about DR.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.040
So why are we talking about DR?

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.230
Well, if you're responsible
for the availability

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.480
of your systems,
then you've probably

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.850
had a conversation
something like this.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.710
How many of you have
lived that picture?

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.290
Yeah, quite a few of you.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.600
So I'm now going
to kind of delve

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.340
into some of the patterns and
things that you can do here.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.260
So you'd be aware that whenever
you start talking about DR,

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.450
there are some Three-Letter
Acronyms, or TLAs,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.420
where you usually start.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.170
I'd like to start
off by distinguishing

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.910
between DR and HA.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.250
Think of HA as being
for micro-disasters.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.790
A single server dies.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.570
A disk fails.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.900
Whereas DR is for
a larger disaster.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.550
A huge chunk of your
production systems break down

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.099
and you need to failover
to a different location.

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.830
Natural disasters
also fall under there.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.330
So there is a relationship
between the two.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.200
And in some cases, particularly
when running your production

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.900
workloads on GCP,
you will see how

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.720
HA influences your DR strategy.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.690
For example, using GCP's
global load balancer

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.100
means that if a region
becomes unavailable,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.740
your application just
keeps on working.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.650
When we talk about DR,
there are two main terms

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.240
that you need to understand.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.570
First there is Recovery
Time Objective, RTO,

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.240
which is directly related
to how long it takes to get

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.850
your application back online.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.880
It is determined by
what strategy you adopt.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.760
This value is usually defined as
part of a larger service level

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.540
agreement.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.990
Secondly, there's Recovery
Point Objective, RPO,

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.750
which relates to the
amount of data at risk.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.240
Ultimately, it boils
down to how much data can

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.190
you actually afford to lose?

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.680
Think of it as the last
backup or checkpoint

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.450
you can roll back to.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.900
This metric will vary based
on the ways the data is used.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.690
For example, frequently
modified user data

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.550
could have an RPO of
just a few minutes,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.050
as you probably
have the transaction

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.500
logs that you can roll back to.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.900
Whereas infrequently
modified data

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.170
may have an RPO of several hours
if you need to restore maybe

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.120
last night's backup.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.580
Note that this metric
describes the length

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.210
of time, not the amount
or quality of the data.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.350
So taken together, these metrics
have a roughly asymptotic

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.630
impact on your
bottom line, which

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.610
means that the smaller
your RTO and RPO

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.310
value, the more your
application will cost to run.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.350
SLA and SLO are
normally conflated,

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.460
but the key here is that the
SLA is the entire agreement

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.280
and specifies what
service is to be provided,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.070
and how it's supported--
times, locations,

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.270
cost performance, penalties,
and responsibilities

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.810
of the parties involved.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.440
Whereas SLO are specific
measurable characteristics

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.320
of the SLA, such as
availability, throughput,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.630
frequency, or quality.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.600
An SLA may contain many SLOs.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.220
We'll keep those
definitions in mind

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.428
as we look at some of the
patterns and implementation

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:21.440
details for the scenarios.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.480
Before we explore the
approaches you could take,

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.070
let's take a quick look
at some of the products

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.050
and key features
that the GCP has

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.260
available to help you implement
your DR strategy no matter

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.710
where your production
workload is.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.880
So it's a little
bit of an eye chart.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:38.720
Cloud Storage.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.550
This is an object store, great
for storage of backup files.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.730
Cloud Load Balancing provides a
single, globally accessible IP

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.370
address to front your
backend instances.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.840
It's global, so your
application could

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.740
have instances running
in Europe and the US,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.930
and your end users would
be directed to the closest

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.360
set of resources.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.971
GCE instances and images,
the workhorse of the cloud.

00:04:03.971 --> 00:04:05.470
These are virtual
machines which you

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.250
can take incremental
backups of, or snapshots of,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.430
that you can copy
across regions.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.730
You can create custom images
with your application.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.640
You can then use them to
launch instances from.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.279
Managed instance groups work
in conjunction with GLB,

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.110
provides a way to
distribute traffic

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.080
to multiple copies of
identically configured groups

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.720
of instances spread
across multiple zones.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.680
Cloud DNS provides
a programmatic way

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.230
to manage your DNS entries.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.916
Deployment Manager allows you
to define your GCP environment

00:04:37.916 --> 00:04:39.540
in a set of templates
that you can then

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.840
use to create complex
environments, and simple ones

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.377
as well, with a single command
repeatedly and consistently.

00:04:46.377 --> 00:04:48.210
Equally, you could tear
the environment down

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.230
with a single command.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.300
Network connectivity options.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.580
To transfer data
to and from GCP,

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.750
you need good connectivity.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.860
So we provide you with choices
in how you can do this.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.144
We have a variety of
connectivity options.

00:05:02.144 --> 00:05:03.560
And it's more than
likely that you

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.530
will choose cloud
interconnect or direct

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:09.910
pairing to connect through to
GCP for your DR configurations.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.610
So you have a reliable link
and we're not dependent

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.550
on the variances of
traversing the internet, where

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.400
there is no control on the
hops between your on-premises

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.870
and GCP.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.960
I think we're in good shape
now to start exploring

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.550
the approaches you can take.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.090
So let's start with looking
at DR when your production

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.790
workload is on GCP,
and start seeing how we

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:35.140
can start using this toolbox.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.930
Before I go on, I'm just
wanting to drill into cloud

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.540
storage in a bit more detail.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.270
There are a number
of storage classes.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.470
The characteristics
of the specific class

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.080
determines the most
appropriate use case.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.730
So in the DR scenario, Nearline
is of particular interest

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.250
to us.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.830
Nearline reduces
your storage costs,

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.200
but access costs are
higher than standard.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.630
It's designed for
backup-type scenarios

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.090
where access is, at
most, once a month.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.190
Ideal for allowing you
to do those DR stress

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.110
tests while keeping costs low.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:13.930
Data is always a good
place to start, I reckon,

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:16.180
so let's look at
data DR techniques

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.430
when your data tier is on GCP.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.350
It's safe to assume
that most of you

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.170
have databases, perhaps several.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:27.861
And likely, you've considered
a tiered storage solution.

00:06:27.861 --> 00:06:29.610
Looking at tiered
storage in this context,

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:33.310
we are focusing on using
it for backups, where

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.880
you have the most recent
backups on faster storage,

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.100
and then you archive
off to backups

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.840
to cheaper, slower storage.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.520
It's a pretty common pattern.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.870
So Ben's session this
afternoon on building hybrid

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.810
cloud tiering with Cloud
Storage for backup and archival

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.680
goes into more depth
on this technique.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.350
Here you can see an illustration
of a typical tiered storage

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.740
setup on the GCP
where we're using

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.500
the characteristics of Cloud
Storage classes to implement.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.480
Inevitably, you'll
need to define

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.521
some rules that describe how
objects move between the tiers.

00:07:08.521 --> 00:07:10.810
Thankfully, GCP makes
it easy by allowing

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.120
you to script a few
simple rules, which

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.040
are known as lifecycle rules.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.750
You could do things
like downgrade

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:22.400
the storage class of an object,
older than 10 days, say.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.690
Delete objects created
before March 1, 2017.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.310
Or maybe keep only the
three most recent versions

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.675
of an object in a bucket
with versioning enabled.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.409
That's only half
the story, though.

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:37.950
And as on-premises,
you need to think

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.680
about what happens when you
need to do a major upgrade

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.660
to the database server, say.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.670
If so, then you will
need to figure out

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.740
how you can get back to a
version on the database server

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.090
that the backups you
stored in Cloud Storage

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.860
are compatible with.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.000
It's not as onerous
as it sounds, though.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.460
OK?

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.380
You actually have some options
about what you can do here.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:02.420
We allow you to create
a custom image that

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.063
has the relevant version
of the database installed.

00:08:05.063 --> 00:08:06.646
Before we go into
that, I want to talk

00:08:06.646 --> 00:08:08.780
a little about snapshots.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:11.390
Snapshots are available
across regions,

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:13.461
which means that if you
snapshot your database,

00:08:13.461 --> 00:08:15.460
you can restore it to a
different region as easy

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.940
as you can restore it
to the same region.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.490
As you could take regular
snapshots of your system,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.790
this also means each
time you upgrade,

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.770
you can roll back
to the snapshot

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.230
with the previous
version if you need to.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.780
As a short-term
strategy, snapshots

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:33.559
are useful for fast recovery.

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:35.900
So back to custom images.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.409
When you launch an instance,
you start from an image.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:43.450
This can be as minimal as
just having the default OS,

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.640
or maybe having
been fully baked,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.625
and having your
application, and there'll

00:08:47.625 --> 00:08:49.210
be [INAUDIBLE] stored on it.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:53.220
Decide where on the
continuum your image fits.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.400
Is it fully baked or do
you want just the OS?

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.960
When choosing your
baking strategy,

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.170
you'll need to consider
things like boot time

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.470
and manageability.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:05.910
Fast boot times are a must
for autoscaling web servers.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:09.210
But a database server may
not need that fast boot time.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.710
Thinking about how
the instance will

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.680
be used in the context
of your DR requirements

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.360
will help you determine what
your starting image should

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:16.860
look like.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.480
Up until now, I've discussed
DR techniques applicable

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.840
when your database is
deployed on a GCE instance.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:31.070
However, GCP has a range of
managed data databases that fit

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.660
a wide variety of use cases.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:38.580
So you choose to
use one of these

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.180
so you don't have to take
on the operational overhead

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.120
of managing them yourselves,
as you do when deploying

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.700
a database on GCE instances.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:51.200
This table lists them together
with some sample backup

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.790
methods.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.180
OK.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.680
I've been talking about
data backup and recovery,

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.780
but that's only half the story.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.930
You also need to consider the
DR strategy for your application

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.820
or service that uses that data.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:06.560
The techniques I've
already discussed,

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.610
like snapshots and the
concept discussed with images,

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.596
are all applicable.

00:10:12.596 --> 00:10:13.970
We need to bring
your application

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.080
and your data recovery
techniques together,

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.400
look at the bigger picture
when designing the strategy.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.640
The typical way to do this is
to think about your RTO and RPO

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.830
values, and then
what DR pattern you

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:29.890
can adopt to meet those values.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:34.070
So DR patterns are considered
to be cold, warm, and hot.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.470
Think of these as being how
ready you are to recover

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.630
from when something goes wrong.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:43.580
Think about what you would
do if you had a puncture.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:47.356
How you deal with it depends
with how prepared you are.

00:10:47.356 --> 00:10:49.730
And we could draw an analogy
with the cold, warm, and hot

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:50.940
patterns.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.520
So you're driving along.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:54.050
You have a puncture.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.790
But you have no spare tire.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.650
So you need to call
someone out to come out

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.920
to you with a replacement tire,
and then they have to fit one.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.530
You have to wait
until they arrive.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:07.320
Moving along the continuum, the
equivalent of a warm pattern

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.030
would be if you
the spare or kit.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:14.625
So yeah, you do have to stop,
but you could fix the tire

00:11:14.625 --> 00:11:15.887
or replace it.

00:11:15.887 --> 00:11:17.220
And then you're on your journey.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.420
So you've had a bit of a break.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:23.550
But if you have run-flat tire,
yeah, you need to slow down.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:24.500
But you don't stop.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:26.950
There's no real impact
on your journey.

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.430
OK?

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:32.430
So it's just basically
the idea of how ready

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:35.580
you are, and therefore, how
quickly you can recover.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.320
So let's look at a
few examples of how

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.190
you can apply these patterns
to production workloads on GCP.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.240
This cold pattern
example requires

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:50.530
you only ever have a single
instance up and running.

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:53.210
Here the serving instance is
part of the instance group,

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.990
and that group is used as a
backend service for an HTTPS

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.620
load balancer.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:00.100
The heartbeat in
snapshot instance

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.540
is part of a managed
instance group.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:06.410
Managed instance groups are a
group of identical instances

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:08.330
which work with load
balancing services

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.160
to distribute network
traffic to all

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.370
of the instances in the group.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:16.270
The managed instance group is
controlled by a GCE autoscaler.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:20.020
The autoscaler is configured
to keep exactly one

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:21.640
instance running at all times.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.420
If it fails, then another
identically configured instance

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.380
is launched in its place.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:30.400
The heartbeat and snapshot
instance runs a cron job

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.720
at regular intervals to
snapshot the serving instance,

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.190
and it also checks the health
of the serving instance

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:38.800
at regular intervals.

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.930
If the heartbeat and
snapshot instance

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.910
detects that the
serving instance has

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.960
been unresponsive for a
specified period of time,

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.570
it instantiates a new serving
instance using the latest

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.460
snapshot, and adds
the new instance

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:55.210
to the managed instance group.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.180
When the new serving
instance comes online,

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.152
the HTTPS load balancer
begins directing traffic

00:13:00.152 --> 00:13:00.985
to the new instance.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:06.600
Looking at this
warm static example,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.800
you could see how we're
making use of Cloud Storage

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.100
to provide a DR
solution for a web

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:14.210
app running on Compute Engine.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:15.890
In the unlikely
event that you are

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.700
unable to serve your application
from Compute Engine instances,

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.590
then you can mitigate
service interruption

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:26.450
by using the Cloud Storage-based
static site on standby.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.640
In the normal
configuration, Cloud DNS

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.590
is configured to point at
this primary application,

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.850
and the standby static
site sits dormant.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:37.310
In the event that the
Compute Engine application

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:39.170
is unable to serve
due to a problem,

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:40.790
you would simply
configure Cloud DNS

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:43.070
to point at the static site.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:44.929
This implementation
of a warm pattern

00:13:44.929 --> 00:13:47.220
is one of those patterns
where the cloud has encouraged

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.060
you to think differently.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:51.920
If you do not need
all those wiggly

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.220
moving parts all the time, this
could be the pattern for you.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:01.190
A hot pattern, when your
production's on GCP,

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:04.430
is basically a
well-architected HA deployment.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.960
In this example, we're making
use of a regional managed

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.510
instance group together with
load balancing and Cloud SQL.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:14.000
As I mentioned earlier,
managed insurance groups

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.220
are groups of
identical instances

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.800
which work with load
balancing services

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.300
to distribute network
traffic to all

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.100
of the instances in the group.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:24.500
In this example, we are using
a regional managed instance

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:25.999
group, so we have
instances that are

00:14:25.999 --> 00:14:28.460
distributed across three zones.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.440
Now, what's really
cool about regional

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.560
managed instance group is
that you get HA in depth.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:36.650
It provides mechanisms to react
to failures at the application

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.750
instance or zone level.

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.670
And you don't have
to manually intervene

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.910
if any of those
scenarios should occur.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:46.130
To address
application-level recovery

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.610
as part of setting up the
managed instance group.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:51.620
HTTP health checks
are configured

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:54.640
that monitor and verify that
services are running properly

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.840
on the instances in that group.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:00.140
If a health check determines
that a service has failed

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.360
on an instance, the
group automatically

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.800
recreates the instance.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.040
Moving up the stack, if
an instance in the group

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.210
unexpectedly stops,
crashes, or is deleted,

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:14.250
the managed instance group
automatically recreates

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:18.530
the instance so it can
resume its processing tasks.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.870
And if for some reason a
zone becomes available,

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:23.520
you do not have to intervene,
as by using a regional managed

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.300
instant group, there will
still be instances available

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.120
serving your application.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.610
The global load
balancer accepts traffic

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:35.660
through a single global
external with IP address,

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.310
and then distributes
traffic according

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:41.050
to forwarding rules you define.

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.730
You set up health checks here to
ensure that new connections are

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.260
only load balanced
to healthy instance

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:48.920
that are up and ready
to receive them.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:52.460
In the data layer,
we have Cloud SQL,

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:53.820
which is configured for HA.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.810
So we have set up a failover
replica in a different zone

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:58.280
from the master.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.890
All changes made to
the data on the master,

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:03.710
including to user
tables, are replicated

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:08.390
to the failover replica using
semi-asynchronous replication.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:12.620
If the zone where the master is
located experiences an outage,

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:16.730
Cloud SQL automatically
fails over to the replica,

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:20.820
and your data continues to
be available to clients.

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:23.910
Existing connections to
the instance are closed,

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:25.560
but your application
can reconnect

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.620
using the same connection
string or IP address,

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.040
as you don't need to update
your application after failover.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.950
After the failover, the
replica becomes the master

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:38.430
and Cloud SQL automatically
creates a new failover replica

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:40.230
in another zone.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.060
This architecture
is a great example

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.414
of why, by using services
that provide HA facilities,

00:16:45.414 --> 00:16:46.830
your application
can automatically

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.390
recover when things go wrong.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:58.140
My favorite tool in the DR
toolbox is Deployment Manager.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.580
It allows you to define
what your environment should

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:03.490
look like using Jinja
templates in Python.

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.380
With a few clicks or a
single gcloud command,

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:09.210
you could bring up an
environment within minutes.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.270
Let's see Deployment
Manager in action,

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.710
bringing up the HA web
application we just looked at.

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.020
I'd like to welcome Oleg.

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:18.819
Oleg.

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.210
One of my colleagues based
out of our Amsterdam office

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:22.905
is going to drive
the demo for us.

00:17:22.905 --> 00:17:25.569
Oleg, thanks.

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:28.349
That's Wizard, by the way.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.547
He's our office dog.

00:17:30.547 --> 00:17:31.130
And his human.

00:17:33.715 --> 00:17:35.550
I did pay him, by the way.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:38.081
I gave him dog treats for
letting me use his picture.

00:17:38.081 --> 00:17:38.580
OK.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.410
So the first thing
Oleg is going to do

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:46.990
is download the set of
Deployment Management templates

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.670
from a bucket, and
he's going to deploy

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.574
it using the gcloud tool.

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:04.800
Deployment Manager uses a
set of declarative templates

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:07.800
that let you consistently
deploy, update,

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:12.250
and delete resources, such as
GCE, GKE, and Cloud Storage,

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:13.510
for example.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:16.560
Typically, you have, at
minimum, a configuration file

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.430
and one or more template files.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:24.570
Schema files are also
usually part of the parcel.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:29.980
They allow you to
declare a set of rules

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:31.920
a configuration file
must meet if it wants

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.160
to use a particular template.

00:18:37.046 --> 00:18:38.420
The templates, as
I've mentioned,

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:42.440
can be written in Jinja
or Python, your choice.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:46.250
Jinja is simpler, but less
powerful than using Python.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:47.720
Using Python gives
you the ability

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:51.389
to programmatically generate
the contents of your templates.

00:18:51.389 --> 00:18:53.180
You could pass variables
to your templates,

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.860
which make them easy to use.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:58.820
We set up the Cloud
SQL backend up earlier,

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:00.890
because in reality, you're
not likely to recreate

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:03.570
your database environment
from scratch in a DR scenario,

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:04.780
so we didn't either.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:05.810
OK?

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:08.199
Bearing in mind, if you don't
have a database ready up

00:19:08.199 --> 00:19:10.740
and running, not only would you
need to create the databases,

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.930
you don't have to
load it with data.

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:15.230
And it would affect how
long it takes to recover.

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:18.630
And that's what
RTO value you have.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.270
It wouldn't be a small value.

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:24.420
In any DR pattern that involves
a database of some kind,

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:27.660
that tends to be the overarching
recovery process that

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:32.080
dictates the achievable RTO.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.826
So can we look at the
autoscaler template, she says.

00:19:34.826 --> 00:19:36.700
So we don't need to
gather around his laptop.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:38.160
That's good.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:39.520
OK.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:42.970
So if you look at
this template file,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:45.900
you'll see that we've declared
the minimum number of replicas

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:49.140
that the autoscaler can
scale down to is three.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:50.250
We can switch to--

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.400
can you switch to the
GCE console, please?

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:52.910
OLEG: Yes.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:54.590
GRACE MOLLISON: OK.

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:58.110
So remember, we said three.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.660
So you could see, we've
got three instances there.

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:04.150
Can you delete one
of them now, Oleg?

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:04.800
Go nuts.

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.190
So as we have autoscaling
on, it will automatically

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:17.150
replace the instance that
Oleg has just deleted.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:20.480
I spoke earlier about HA in
depth using managed instance

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.980
groups together with autoscaling
of groups being a key tool.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:28.040
So here we're demonstrating
this capability

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:30.260
at the instance failure level.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.960
But equally, we could set
up a HTTP health check

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.050
that we set up
against the group.

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:37.740
In fact, it takes the actual
application one or the instance

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.670
failed, it would automatically
create a new instance

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:42.790
and delete the failing one.

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:43.480
OK.

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.682
OLEG: We did.

00:20:44.682 --> 00:20:45.890
GRACE MOLLISON: Thanks, Oleg.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.600
If you're using a load
balancers as well,

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:49.850
you can also set up
health checks here too.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:51.420
But load balancer
helps [? tabs ?]

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:53.600
who just stop serving traffic.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.150
They don't actually
remove the instance.

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:57.560
They don't delete them.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:58.380
OK?

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:00.920
So you need to
understand how to set up

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:02.600
both types of health checks.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:05.120
The rule of thumb is be more
conservative with setting up

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:09.280
health checks against the
managed instant group.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.715
So as you can see, the
replacement instance

00:21:12.715 --> 00:21:13.520
is coming up.

00:21:16.262 --> 00:21:17.422
OLEG: Yep, it's up.

00:21:17.422 --> 00:21:19.130
GRACE MOLLISON: As
there isn't actually--

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:20.129
we got there in the end.

00:21:20.129 --> 00:21:21.207
Sorry about the delay.

00:21:21.207 --> 00:21:23.415
As there isn't actually
anything wrong with the zone.

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:25.874
The replacement instance got
launched into the same zone

00:21:25.874 --> 00:21:27.040
as the one we were deleting.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.100
So anyone who was paying
attention and notice the zone,

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:30.380
you would see it's
put it straight back

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:31.130
into the same one.

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:36.410
So after that, we could easily
tear down the deployment

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:37.990
by just deleting it.

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:39.680
So, OK.

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:43.580
So this is great if you're
doing test stress of your DR

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:45.530
environment, which
you should do.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.560
So I understand, having
been there myself,

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:53.210
is the fact that doing
full-scale tests are difficult.

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:54.909
This just makes life easier.

00:21:54.909 --> 00:21:56.450
You've seen it
[INAUDIBLE] and you've

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.310
fired up the environment.

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:00.000
We pulled it down as well.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.870
So you're actually
investing lots of stuff

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:05.030
that staying there that
you don't use very often.

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.530
And then you're worried because
you don't use it very often,

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:10.116
why hasn't it come up?

00:22:10.116 --> 00:22:11.890
So moving on.

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:13.369
OK.

00:22:13.369 --> 00:22:14.274
OLEG: Bye-bye.

00:22:14.274 --> 00:22:16.190
GRACE MOLLISON: OK, thank
you very much, Oleg.

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:17.850
Thank you.

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:18.650
Thanks, Oleg.

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:19.150
OK.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:22.130
So moving on, let's look at DR
where production is on premises

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.870
and your recovery site
is Cloud Platform.

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:30.230
So I'll be covering when your
production is just on GSP.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.150
When you're using
GCP as the recovery

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:35.030
site for your
on-premises workload,

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:38.000
there are a few key things
you need to consider.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:39.670
The initial question
usually being,

00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:42.200
how do you connect to GCP?

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:45.260
GCP provides a number of ways
to connect from on premises.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:47.510
Cloud interconnect
being the ideal way

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:50.300
to provide a consistent,
reliable link.

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:52.220
You need to take into
consideration factors

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.300
such as the bandwidth
between you and the carrier

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:55.910
interconnect
provider, as well as

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:59.450
the actual bandwidth provided
by the provider directly to GCP.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:03.120
Think about what other data
will be used in that link.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.580
Consider how much data
has been transferred

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.850
and how long have you got
to meet any RTO values.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.259
You can see from this table--

00:23:11.259 --> 00:23:13.050
by the way, which isn't
taking into account

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.060
any external factors at all--

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:18.240
that bandwidth
availability does have

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:22.900
a direct effect on the time
it takes to transfer data.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:26.130
Don't forget to take into
account your security controls.

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:28.260
You may determine that
you want total control

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:29.970
of your encryption
keys, so factor

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:33.450
in the end-to-end
encryption/decryption process.

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:34.660
Who would have access?

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:37.890
Think about authentication
and authorization mechanisms.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.830
How much can you automate?

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:42.270
Remember, everything
is an API, so you

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:45.420
don't need to rely on an
actual, probably sleepy, human

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:48.050
to start bringing up
online your DR site.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.602
I know whenever I had to
instigate a DR scenario,

00:23:51.602 --> 00:23:53.310
it was always in the
middle of the night.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:56.580
I was always half asleep.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:59.280
So when taking a traditional
approach to DR on premises,

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:02.370
it typically involves,
somewhere in the process, people

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:05.400
driving fans and
swapping tapes around.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.420
The types usually-- or
should I say, hopefully,

00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:10.360
being the off-site backup.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.524
Remember-- [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.990
Remember this?

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:20.390
Well, we can use the same
tiered storage approach

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:23.030
to replace the need
for tapes, and fans,

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:25.070
moving tapes on and off site.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:28.160
We can replace the source to
be your on premises storage

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:31.010
appliance, for example.

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:35.870
So here you need to set
up connectivity to GCP.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:39.590
So in this example, we're
using GCP's cloud interconnect

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:40.490
service.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.770
You can then implement
a process to copy data

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:46.610
from the storage device
to a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:50.960
This process can be very simple
using, say, the GSU2 command.

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:54.950
You may also want to just
set up a GCS lifecycle rule,

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:58.640
like we described before,
to delete data and move

00:24:58.640 --> 00:24:59.780
to a cheaper storage class.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.080
Here's an example
of a cold pattern.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:07.920
In this example, we have
practically nothing running

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:10.155
in your target GCP project.

00:25:10.155 --> 00:25:12.280
When there's a problem that
prevents the production

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:14.800
environment running
production workloads,

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.600
you deploy a Deployment
Manager template

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:18.550
to create an environment
capable of running

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:21.370
production workloads.

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:24.910
Note that we were trickling
data into Cloud Storage as part

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:27.250
of that production environment.

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:28.690
You'll need to
implement processes

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:32.120
to restore the applications
to all the instances spun up.

00:25:32.120 --> 00:25:34.570
So think back to the
continuum of images

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:35.944
we spoke about earlier.

00:25:35.944 --> 00:25:37.360
You'll also need
to think of a way

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:40.300
to transfer data from Cloud
Storage to the database

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:43.440
you spin up in GCP when
you invoke your DR plan,

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:46.690
and start serving production
traffic from your GCP

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:47.860
environment.

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:51.430
This pattern may be the
most cost-effective,

00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:55.240
but it has the highest RPO
and RTO values in the patterns

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:57.190
and requires the most
operational effort

00:25:57.190 --> 00:25:58.000
to recover from.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:05.420
Moving on, here's what a warm
standby setup could look like.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.650
Here we have a multi-tiered
application running on premises

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:12.160
while using the minimal
recovery suite on GCP.

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.750
Ignore the Cloud SQL
instance for a moment.

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:16.720
I'll come back to that.

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:19.270
You could see that a
database server instance

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:21.640
is running on the GCP side.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:24.040
The instance must
run at all times

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:27.850
that it can receive replicated
transactions via asynchronous

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:31.630
or semi-asynchronous
replication techniques.

00:26:31.630 --> 00:26:33.980
To reduce cost, you
could run the database

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:36.790
on the smallest machine type
capable of running the database

00:26:36.790 --> 00:26:38.060
service.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:39.880
As this would be a
long-running instance,

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:43.450
sustained user
discounts will apply.

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:46.240
When the on-premises
application needs to failover,

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:49.450
you can make the database
system on GCP production

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:53.490
ready by destroying the
small database instance,

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:56.790
making sure to keep the
persistent disk containing

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.340
your database system intact.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:00.872
If your system is
on the boot disk,

00:27:00.872 --> 00:27:03.080
you will need to set the
autodelete state on the disk

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:06.700
to false before
destroying the instance.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:08.790
Create a new instance
using the machine type that

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:11.850
has appropriate resources for
handling the production load

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:15.210
and attach the persistent disk
containing your database system

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.570
to the new instance.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:19.577
In the event of disaster,
the monitoring service

00:27:19.577 --> 00:27:22.160
will be triggered to spin up the
web tier and application tier

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:24.140
instances in GCP.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:26.220
You can then adjust
the Cloud DNS record

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.220
to point to the web tier,
which in this example,

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:31.280
points to the external IP
address of the HTTPS load

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:34.960
balancing service.

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:35.460
OK.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:38.420
So coming back to that
Cloud SQL instance,

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.630
if your on-premises
database is MySQL,

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:43.270
you can use the Cloud SQL
replica instead of the VM

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.850
instance, and data
will replicate directly

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:49.330
from the external master
to the Cloud SQL replica.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:52.510
For smaller RTO values, you
could adjust the above strategy

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:55.690
by keeping all of the Compute
Engine instances operational,

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:57.980
but not receiving traffic.

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:01.840
This is not cost-effective,
but may be an option

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:03.650
that meets your needs.

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:06.100
You may want a ready to take
over production workload

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.110
database instance,
so you can omit

00:28:08.110 --> 00:28:10.330
the resizing of your instance.

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:12.370
In this pattern,
you'll notice that you

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:14.990
have to replicate the
data to your database.

00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:18.160
This is one way of ensuring your
DR failover database is kept up

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:20.970
to date.

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:23.550
There are other options for
keeping your recovery database

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:24.480
up to date.

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:26.460
The method you choose
will have a direct impact

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.080
on your RPO value.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.730
You may choose to apply
backup and transactional logs

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.820
as your data recovery strategy.

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:35.420
This means you do need to make
copies of the transaction log

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:37.740
files, as well as the backups.

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:40.590
You need to regularly restore
backups and apply transaction

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.590
log files to your
recovery database,

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:46.860
and test that it acts just
like the production database.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:49.530
Exactly how you
implement this depends

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:52.170
on the flavor of
database you are using.

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:54.399
Maybe you have a read
copy that can be promoted.

00:28:54.399 --> 00:28:56.190
It's similar to the
techniques we discussed

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:59.069
when using HA Cloud SQL.

00:28:59.069 --> 00:29:01.027
You could choose to keep
a copy of the raw data

00:29:01.027 --> 00:29:03.390
on cloud storage
and rebuild them,

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:07.970
assuming your RTO was
long enough to allow that.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:10.490
What happens when you've fallen
over to your DR database?

00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:12.610
How do you then get
your production database

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:13.790
back up to date?

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:15.950
In many cases, it's
just the reverse process

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:19.100
you'd be following to keep
your DR database up to date.

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:23.480
Data recovery is tricky, and you
need to run regular fire drills

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.930
and be totally confident that
you can happily run production,

00:29:26.930 --> 00:29:29.410
whether you are using your
production database or your DR

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:30.170
database.

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:32.480
Take advantage of Cloud
Deployment Manager

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:34.790
to spin up your
failover application,

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:37.100
point it at your DR
database to validate.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:38.900
And once you're done,
you can tear down

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:41.090
your replica
environment until it's

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:42.460
needed for the next fire drill.

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:51.040
If you have very small
RTO and RPO values,

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:53.570
then you could take
that warm pattern

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:57.920
and modify it so it's just
an HA across with GCP, which

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:00.890
gives you a hot pattern,
as both on premises and GCP

00:30:00.890 --> 00:30:03.770
are serving production traffic.

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.200
The key differences from
the previous pattern

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:08.430
being that everything is
running in production mode

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:10.780
and serving production traffic.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:13.400
The GCE instances are
part of a managed instance

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:17.960
group behind a load balancer,
and the GCP database instance

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:20.210
is production ready.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:24.170
If you do choose to implement
this type of hybrid approach,

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:28.250
be sure to consider a DNS
service that supports weighted

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:32.600
routing when routing traffic
to the two production

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:34.490
environments, so that
you are able to deliver

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:36.560
the same application from both.

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:38.730
In the event that one
environment becomes available,

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:43.040
you can then disable the DNS
routing from the unavailable

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:45.050
environment.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:46.790
So we've discussed
several examples

00:30:46.790 --> 00:30:50.570
of using production workloads
in GCP and on premises.

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:54.320
I'm just going to discuss
briefly now what techniques you

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:56.730
could adopt when you're running
production across clouds.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:02.990
If your application involves
virtual machines running

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:05.180
across different
clouds, again, you

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:07.070
have to figure out
where on the continuum

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:10.000
your images are for both clouds.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:13.350
You'd want to use tolling
that works across them both.

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:15.620
If it's a fully
configured image required,

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:17.520
consider something
like Packer, which

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:20.340
could create identical machine
images for multiple platforms

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:23.070
from a single
configuration file.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:24.840
You could use something
like Terraform,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:30.610
which it's a cloud-neutral
templating tool.

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:34.730
So you might want to--

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.670
if you're just running on
GCP, use the native tools.

00:31:37.670 --> 00:31:39.170
If you're running
across clouds, you

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:41.886
need to find something that
runs across both of them.

00:31:41.886 --> 00:31:43.760
You could use configuration
management tools,

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:46.400
such as Chef, Puppet,
Ansible, SaltStack

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:49.830
to configure your instances.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:51.300
Or even to configure
the instances

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:54.150
that you'll use to create
the images you'll be using.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.070
That's a very common
technique, and it

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:58.190
keeps everything consistent.

00:31:58.190 --> 00:32:02.040
If you need to, you could
set up a VPN between clouds.

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:03.239
If you're using containers--

00:32:03.239 --> 00:32:04.530
you know what I'm going to say.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.238
There's only one thing
that needs saying.

00:32:06.238 --> 00:32:08.750
It's Kubernetes.

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:12.140
With Kubernetes, you
confederate across clouds.

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:18.380
You can use GKE, which is
Cloud Platform's fully managed

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:20.360
Kubernetes environment.

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:22.970
And then on, say,
Azure or AWS, you

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:26.120
can use Kubernetes on there.

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:27.875
There are other
techniques and solutions

00:32:27.875 --> 00:32:30.650
to the ones I've just discussed
that you can implement

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:32.930
for multiple cloud scenarios.

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:36.740
For example, if you want to
replicate data from AWS S3

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:39.350
to Cloud Storage, then you
can do this easily as well,

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:43.010
maybe by using Boto, which
is a Python tool designed

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:47.060
to work with AWS, which also
supports Cloud Storage There's

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:49.337
plenty of ways to do that.

00:32:49.337 --> 00:32:50.420
It's not going to be easy.

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:52.720
I'm not going to deny
it, but you can do this.

00:32:56.710 --> 00:33:01.450
So just to recap, why using
GCP is the destination

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:04.180
you should be thinking
of for your DR needs.

00:33:04.180 --> 00:33:07.540
It improves your TCO.

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:09.380
It's easy to carry
out the fire drill

00:33:09.380 --> 00:33:11.740
so you will have a greater
degree of confidence

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:14.920
that in the unfortunate
situation you have a disaster,

00:33:14.920 --> 00:33:17.620
then like Wizard
here, your ops team

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:21.310
will be calmly executing
the recovery process.

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:24.400
Also, you don't need to
keep rethinking patterns.

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:28.060
They apply no matter where your
production workload is running.

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:33.420
So there you have it.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:39.470
If you're thinking of DR, either
to revamp your current solution

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:42.990
or want you to actually
implement a new DR solution,

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.250
hopefully we're giving
you plenty of ideas.

00:33:45.250 --> 00:33:48.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

